.TH HD-REPO 7 "October 2026" "hd-repo" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-repo \- the hyprdebian package repository
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-repo
turns a Debian or hyprdebian system into the hyprdebian package repository.
It holds packages that are not in Debian, for the suites
.B trixie
and
.BR unstable ,
signs them, and serves them over HTTP with
.BR nginx (8).
It only hosts packages; they are built elsewhere.
.PP
Installing the package sets everything up:
.IP \(bu 2
the user
.BR repo ,
which uploads packages and maintains the repository;
.IP \(bu 2
an inbox per suite in
.IR /var/lib/hd-repo/inbox ;
.IP \(bu 2
the repository in
.IR /srv/hd-repo ;
.IP \(bu 2
the signing key in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/hd-repo/gnupg ,
generated on the first installation when there is none;
.IP \(bu 2
the nginx site
.IR /etc/nginx/sites-available/hd-repo ,
which serves
.I /srv/hd-repo
on port 80 in place of the default site of nginx.
.PP
The first, empty indices of both suites are made and signed during the
installation, so the repository works as soon as the installation is done.
.SH SETTING UP
.B repo
logs in with
.BR ssh (1)
using a key; it has no password.
Add the public keys of the people who may upload packages to
.IR /var/lib/hd-repo/.ssh/authorized_keys ,
owned by
.B repo
with mode 0600:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo install \-o repo \-g repo \-m 0600 id_ed25519.pub \e
    /var/lib/hd-repo/.ssh/authorized_keys
.fi
.RE
.PP
The SSH server is recommended, not required, by
.BR hd-repo ;
install
.B openssh-server
when it is missing.
.SH ADDING PACKAGES
Copy the packages to the inbox of their suite, then run
.BR hd-repo-update (1)
as
.BR repo :
.PP
.RS
.nf
scp foo_1.0_amd64.deb repo@repo.example:inbox/unstable/
ssh repo@repo.example hd-repo-update
.fi
.RE
.PP
The inboxes are
.I inbox/trixie
and
.I inbox/unstable
in the home directory of
.BR repo .
A package built for trixie goes in the first, one built for unstable in the
second; a package that fits both is copied to both.
.PP
.B hd-repo-update
checks every package in the inboxes, moves them into the pool of their suite,
replacing older versions of the same package, and makes and signs the indices.
A package that is not valid, or not for amd64 or all, stops the update before
anything changes.
.SH USING THE REPOSITORY
A client needs the public key and an apt source.
Download the key from the repository:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo /usr/lib/apt/apt\-helper download\-file \e
    http://repo.example/hyprdebian\-archive\-keyring.pgp \e
    /usr/share/keyrings/hyprdebian\-archive\-keyring.pgp
.fi
.RE
.PP
and add
.IR /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hyprdebian.local.sources :
.PP
.RS
.nf
Types: deb
URIs: http://repo.example/
Suites: unstable
Components: main
Signed\-By: /usr/share/keyrings/hyprdebian\-archive\-keyring.pgp
Architectures: amd64
.fi
.RE
.PP
For trixie, set
.B Suites
to
.BR trixie .
On a HyprDebian system,
.BR hd-link-repo (8)
does both.
.PP
The key is downloaded over the same connection as the packages, so it does
not protect against someone who can change that traffic.
It makes sure that every client keeps getting its packages from the
repository it was set up with.
.SH LAYOUT
.RS
.nf
/srv/hd\-repo/
  hyprdebian\-archive\-keyring.pgp    the public key
  pool/trixie/main/                 the packages of trixie
  pool/unstable/main/               the packages of unstable
  dists/trixie/                     the indices of trixie
  dists/sid/                        the indices of unstable
  dists/stable \-> trixie
  dists/unstable \-> sid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each directory in
.I dists
holds
.IR Release ,
its signatures
.I InRelease
and
.IR Release.gpg ,
and
.IR main/binary\-amd64/Packages(.gz) .
.PP
The suites have their own pools, so a package built for one suite never
replaces the one built for the other.
Clients may ask for trixie by its codename or as
.BR stable ,
and for unstable by its name or as
.BR sid .
.SH ZFS
When
.I /srv/hd-repo
is a ZFS dataset of its own,
.B hd-repo-update
takes a snapshot of it before it adds packages, so an update can be undone
with
.BR "zfs rollback" .
The installation lets
.B repo
take those snapshots when the dataset already exists.
When the dataset is made later, allow it by hand:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo zfs allow \-u repo snapshot,mount rpool/hd\-repo
.fi
.RE
.SH THE SIGNING KEY
Every client trusts the key in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/hd-repo/gnupg .
It has no passphrase, so updates can sign without asking for one.
Back it up: with a new key,
.B apt update
fails on every client until the new public key is downloaded on it, as in
.BR "USING THE REPOSITORY" .
.PP
Removing the package keeps everything.
Purging it removes the user
.BR repo ,
its home directory with the inboxes, and the nginx site, but keeps
.I /srv/hd-repo
and the key, so a new installation takes them over.
.SH HTTPS
The repository is served over HTTP.
To serve it over HTTPS as well, add a
.B server
block that listens on port 443 with the certificate and
.B root /srv/hd-repo
to
.IR /etc/nginx/sites-available/hd-repo ,
and change
.B http://
to
.B https://
in the sources of the clients.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /srv/hd-repo
The repository, served by nginx.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-repo
The home directory of
.BR repo .
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-repo/inbox/trixie
.TQ
.I /var/lib/hd-repo/inbox/unstable
The inboxes.
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/hd-repo/gnupg
The signing key.
.TP
.I /etc/nginx/sites-available/hd-repo
The nginx site, a conffile.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-repo-update (1),
.BR apt-ftparchive (1),
.BR hd-link-repo (8),
.BR sources.list (5),
.BR nginx (8),
.BR zfs-allow (8)
